%% CASE Orion Foods bakery line modernisation
Orion Foods is modernising a bakery production line in a 14-month hybrid project. The new **equipment** is supplied and installed by a vendor under a fixed-price contract with a baselined schedule. The **line-control software** is developed in two-week sprints by Orion's internal team.

- A new national food-safety regulation will require **automated temperature logging** at every production stage, starting next year.
- The vendor's main component factory is in a region hit by floods. The team estimates a **30% probability** of a two-month delay that would cost Orion about **$150,000**. An alternative supplier could deliver the components on time for an extra **$30,000**, paid up front.
- Production supervisors ask to move the packaging station to the other side of the hall, which would change the installation layout agreed with the vendor.
- Line workers are anxious about the automated equipment, and rumours of job losses are spreading.
@@ BE2 | Hybrid
How should the project manager address the new food-safety regulation?
A) Assess it with experts, then add backlog items and raise change requests as needed
B) Wait until the regulation takes effect next year and then upgrade the line in a separate project
C) Ask the vendor to add temperature logging free of charge, since compliance is the vendor's problem
D) Treat temperature logging as a nice-to-have feature that the team can add if time allows later
= A
? Mandatory requirements must be understood and built in. Software changes go into the backlog; changes to the contracted equipment scope go through change control. Waiting or treating compliance as optional creates legal and rework risk.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid
Based on expected monetary value, what should the project manager recommend about the component supply risk?
A) Use the alternative supplier, since $30,000 is less than the $45,000 expected cost of the delay
B) Accept the risk, since a 30% probability means the delay is unlikely to happen at all
C) Use the alternative supplier only if the delay actually happens, since prevention is too expensive
D) Accept the risk, since the $150,000 impact is smaller than the contract value
= A
? EMV of the threat = 0.30 × $150,000 = $45,000. Paying $30,000 to remove the threat is cost-effective. Waiting until the delay occurs would be too late to use the alternative.

@@ BE3 | Hybrid
How should the request to move the packaging station be handled?
A) Raise a change request and analyse the impact on layout, schedule, cost and contract
B) Ask the vendor's installers to move the station during installation, since the change is small
C) Reject the request, since the installation layout has already been agreed with the vendor
D) Add the request to the software team's product backlog for the product owner to prioritise
= A
? The layout is part of the baselined, contract-based scope. The change must be analysed and approved through change control, including contract implications.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid
What should the project manager do about the line workers' anxiety?
A) Involve the workers early with demonstrations, hands-on training and honest communication about jobs
B) Tell the workers that their concerns are unfounded and that the equipment will be installed anyway
C) Avoid discussing the equipment with the workers until installation is complete to prevent rumours
D) Ask the supervisors to stop the rumours by warning workers against spreading false information
= A
? Supporting organisational change means addressing concerns openly, involving people and building their capability. Dismissing or suppressing concerns increases resistance.
%% END CASE

@@ BE1 | Predictive
In a large predictive project, which decision is most appropriately made by the steering committee rather than the project manager?
A) Approving the transition to the next phase at a phase gate
B) Assigning daily tasks to members of the project team
C) Updating the issue log after a weekly status meeting
D) Choosing the format of the weekly team status report
= A
? Steering committees provide strategic direction and approve major decisions such as phase transitions. Day-to-day management belongs to the project manager.

@@ BE1 | Agile
Within an agile governance model, who decides the order of the product backlog, and within what limits?
A) The product owner, within organisational guardrails
B) The sponsor, who must approve every change to the order of the backlog
C) The developers, who choose the items that are most interesting to build
D) The scrum master, who balances the requests of all the stakeholders
= A
? The product owner is accountable for backlog order, operating within agreed guardrails such as budget, strategy and compliance requirements.

@@ BE1 | Hybrid
A vendor delivers a hardware module under contract while the internal team integrates it with agile software. Who should have the authority to formally accept the vendor's module?
A) The acceptance authority named in the contract and governance plan
B) The scrum master, since the module will be used by the agile software team
C) Any developer who tests the module and finds that it works correctly
D) The vendor's project manager, after completing the vendor's own tests
= A
? Formal acceptance of contracted deliverables follows the authority defined in the contract and governance framework.

@@ BE1 | Predictive
At a phase gate, the project is behind schedule, but the business case remains strong. Which decision outcome is available to the steering committee?
A) Continue with modifications, such as a revised plan
B) Approve the project charter for the first time, as at initiation
C) Transfer the project to another sponsor automatically
D) Close the gate without making any decision until the next review
= A
? Phase gate outcomes include continue, continue with modifications, repeat the phase or terminate. A strong business case with schedule issues often leads to continuation with a revised plan.

@@ BE1 | Hybrid
The organisation's PMO wants to evolve from enforcing templates to helping teams deliver value with both predictive and agile approaches. Which model best describes this evolution?
A) A value delivery office that coaches teams and focuses on outcomes
B) A directive PMO that manages all projects and project managers directly
C) A controlling PMO that adds more mandatory templates for agile teams
D) A supportive PMO that stops providing any guidance or standards
= A
? A value delivery office (agile PMO) shifts the focus from compliance to enabling value, coaching teams and supporting tailored ways of working.

@@ BE1 | Agile | multi
Which two elements should a project governance framework define? (Choose two.)
A) Approval thresholds for decisions and changes
B) Escalation procedures for issues and risks
C) The team's preferred lunch schedule
D) The coding style guide for each developer
E) The colours used in the project dashboard
= A, B
? Governance defines decision rights, thresholds and escalation paths. Coding styles and cosmetic choices belong to the team's working agreements.

@@ BE2 | Agile
The data protection regulation gives customers the right to have their personal data erased. The product does not yet support this. What should the team do?
A) Consult the data protection officer and add prioritised erasure backlog items
B) Delete customer data manually whenever a request arrives, without changing the product
C) Wait until the first customer request arrives before deciding what to build
D) Ask the legal department to tell customers that erasure is not technically possible
= A
? Legal obligations are translated into product capabilities with expert advice and prioritised in the backlog. Ad hoc manual processes or misleading customers are inappropriate.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
A building inspection finds that the fire escape stairs do not comply with the building code. The client wants to open the building next week. What should the project manager do?
A) Correct the non-compliance and arrange a re-inspection before opening
B) Open the building as planned and correct the stairs during the first month
C) Ask the inspector to approve the stairs temporarily, given the deadline
D) Ask the client to sign a waiver accepting responsibility for the stairs
= A
? Safety and building code compliance cannot be waived by the client or bypassed. The defect must be corrected and re-inspected.

@@ BE2 | Hybrid
The team plans to share encryption software with developers in another country. A colleague mentions that export controls may apply. What should the project manager do?
A) Confirm export control requirements with legal experts first
B) Share the software, since it is internal to the company and not being sold to anyone
C) Ask the developers abroad to download the software themselves from the internet
D) Rename the software so that it is not recognised as encryption technology
= A
? Export control laws may apply even within one company. Legal advice must be obtained before transfer; circumventing the rules is unlawful.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
The contract requires monthly progress reports in a specific format, but the team stopped producing them three months ago to save time. What should the project manager do?
A) Resume the required reports and discuss the gap openly with the customer
B) Continue without the reports, since the customer has not complained so far
C) Produce three backdated reports and send them as if they had been sent on time
D) Ask the customer to remove the reporting clause from the contract retroactively
= A
? Contractual obligations must be met. Transparency about the gap maintains trust; backdating documents would be dishonest.

@@ BE2 | Agile
Internal auditors ask the agile team for evidence that requirements were tested before release. The team has never produced such documentation. What is the best approach?
A) Build lightweight test evidence into the definition of done
B) Produce a large set of documents once a year, just before the audit
C) Tell the auditors that agile teams do not produce documentation
D) Ask the product owner to sign a statement that everything was tested
= A
? Compliance evidence can be generated continuously as part of the definition of done, without heavy documentation.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
The sponsor asks the project manager to add a feature immediately and says no paperwork is needed because the sponsor is funding the project. What should the project manager do?
A) Assess the impact and record the change through the change control process
B) Add the feature immediately, since the sponsor has the authority to change scope
C) Refuse the request, since the scope baseline has already been approved
D) Ask the team to add the feature without updating any of the baselines
= A
? Even sponsor-approved changes need impact analysis and documentation so that baselines, plans and stakeholders stay aligned.

@@ BE3 | Agile
After user research, the product owner decides to change the product goal for the next release. What should happen?
A) The product owner updates the backlog and informs stakeholders of the new goal
B) The team submits a formal change request to the change control board for approval
C) The team finishes the current backlog before considering the new product goal
D) The scrum master decides whether the product goal may be changed this release
= A
? In Scrum, the product owner owns the product goal and backlog and adapts them based on learning, keeping stakeholders informed.

@@ BE3 | Hybrid
A critical security vulnerability requires an emergency patch to production within 24 hours. The normal change process takes a week. What should happen?
A) Use the emergency change procedure in the change management plan
B) Wait for the normal weekly change board meeting to approve the patch
C) Apply the patch without any record, since the situation is urgent
D) Ask the vendor to decide whether the patch should be applied
= A
? Good change management plans include an emergency procedure: fast approval, then documentation and review afterwards.

@@ BE3 | Hybrid
After a change was approved, a team member asks why the schedule, budget and test plans were all updated too. What is the best explanation?
A) Integrated change control keeps all affected plans and baselines consistent
B) Every change requires the whole project management plan to be rewritten from scratch
C) The change control board updates documents at random to keep the team informed
D) Only the scope baseline should have changed, so the other updates were a mistake
= A
? Changes ripple across scope, schedule, cost, quality and risk. Integrated change control updates every affected component.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
Two versions of the same design specification are being used on site, and the contractors are building different things. Which practice should have prevented this?
A) Configuration management
B) Resource levelling
C) Benchmarking
D) Stakeholder mapping
= A
? Configuration management controls versions of specifications and documents so everyone works from the approved version.

@@ BE4 | Agile
Developers report that builds on their laptops take 40 minutes, slowing every story. They have already tried the usual optimisations. What should the scrum master do?
A) Make the case for faster build infrastructure and pursue it with management
B) Ask the developers to start more stories in parallel while their builds are running
C) Accept the slow builds as a constraint and reduce the sprint commitment permanently
D) Ask the developers to buy faster laptops with their own money and claim them later
= A
? Removing organisational impediments, such as inadequate tools, is a servant-leader responsibility; quantifying the productivity loss supports the business case.

@@ BE4 | Predictive
An issue was escalated to the operations director three weeks ago, but no decision has been made, and the issue is now delaying critical work. What should the project manager do?
A) Follow up, explain the growing impact and ask for a decision by a set date
B) Make the decision personally, since the director has not responded within three weeks
C) Remove the issue from the issue log, since it has already been escalated to the director
D) Wait for the director's decision without further contact to avoid seeming impatient
= A
? Issues are tracked to closure. Following up with clear impact information and a deadline helps the decision-maker act.

@@ BE4 | Hybrid
The agile team's integration testing is blocked because another department's predictive project has delayed an interface it promised to deliver. What should the project manager do?
A) Coordinate with the other project manager, escalating through governance if needed
B) Ask the agile team to build the interface themselves without informing the other department
C) Wait until the other project delivers the interface before testing anything further
D) Report the other department to the sponsor as the cause of the team's delay
= A
? Cross-project dependencies are resolved by coordinating with peers first, then escalating through governance if necessary.

@@ BE4 | Agile
During the daily scrum, a developer mentions an impediment: the test data server is down. What should the scrum master do during the meeting?
A) Note it and work on removing it right after the daily scrum
B) Stop the daily scrum and solve the problem with all developers present
C) Ask the developer to solve the problem alone after the meeting
D) Ignore it, since the developer did not ask for help explicitly
= A
? The daily scrum stays short and focused; impediments raised there are addressed immediately afterwards by the scrum master or the relevant people.

@@ BE4 | Hybrid
The business approver for several pending design decisions has resigned, and the decisions are now blocking work. What should the project manager do?
A) Use the governance framework to find an interim approver
B) Make the design decisions personally until a new approver is appointed
C) Stop all work that depends on the decisions until a replacement is hired
D) Ask the team to choose the designs they consider best without approval
= A
? Governance should define delegation and escalation. The PM uses it to restore decision-making rather than overstepping authority or stalling.

@@ BE4 | Agile | multi
Which two situations are impediments that the scrum master should help remove? (Choose two.)
A) A month-long approval process for buying a test tool the team needs
B) The team lacks access to a test environment
C) Deciding which stories to include in the sprint
D) Writing the code for the most complex story
E) Ordering the product backlog by value
= A, B
? Impediments are obstacles to the team's progress, especially organisational ones. Selecting and ordering work, and writing code, are the responsibilities of the developers and the product owner.

@@ BE5 | Predictive
The team assumes that a key subcontractor will be available in March. Which risk identification technique specifically examines whether such assumptions might be inaccurate?
A) Assumption and constraint analysis
B) Monte Carlo schedule simulation
C) Earned value performance analysis
D) Resource levelling and smoothing
= A
? Assumption and constraint analysis explores the validity of assumptions and identifies risks from inaccurate, unstable or incomplete ones.

@@ BE5 | Agile
How do agile teams typically manage identified risks within their normal workflow?
A) Risk responses become backlog items prioritised alongside features
B) Risks are managed only by the project sponsor outside the team's workflow
C) Risks are recorded once at the start and reviewed only at release closure
D) Risks are ignored, since short iterations eliminate all project risks
= A
? A risk-adjusted backlog makes risk responses visible work, prioritised together with features.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid
The risk management plan sets a threshold: any risk with a potential delay of more than four weeks must be reported to the steering committee. A newly identified risk could cause a six-week delay. What should the project manager do?
A) Analyse the risk, plan a response and report it to the steering committee
B) Plan a response but keep the risk within the team to avoid alarming anyone
C) Ignore the threshold, since the risk has not yet occurred
D) Close the risk, since six weeks is within the overall schedule reserve
= A
? Thresholds trigger defined actions; this risk exceeds the reporting threshold, so it must be escalated with a response plan.

@@ BE5 | Predictive
The risk owner for a supplier risk asks a buyer in the procurement team to arrange a backup contract. What is the buyer's role in this response?
A) Risk action owner for a specific response action
B) Risk owner who is accountable for the entire risk
C) Sponsor of the project's risk management process
D) Member of the change control board for this risk
= A
? The risk owner remains accountable for the risk; others can be assigned specific response actions as risk action owners.

@@ BE5 | Agile
The client offers a substantial bonus if the first release goes live a month early. The team decides to dedicate its most experienced developers to the release and remove all other work to make the early date certain. Which strategy is this?
A) Exploit
B) Enhance
C) Share
D) Accept
= A
? Taking actions to make an opportunity certain to occur is exploiting it; enhancing would only increase its likelihood or impact.

@@ BE5 | Predictive
Two risks have the same probability and impact scores. One could occur next month; the other not for a year. Which risk parameter helps decide which to address first?
A) Proximity
B) Connectivity
C) Detectability
D) Dormancy
= A
? Proximity measures how soon a risk could affect objectives. A risk that could occur next month generally needs attention sooner.

@@ BE6 | Predictive
Lessons learned from the first phase show that duration estimates were consistently 20% too optimistic. What should the project manager do for the next phase?
A) Use the lessons to calibrate the estimating approach
B) Keep the same estimating approach, since every phase is different
C) Add 20% to every estimate without discussing it with the team
D) Record the lesson for future projects only, not for this one
= A
? Lessons learned should improve the current project as well as future ones. Calibrating the approach with the team addresses the root cause.

@@ BE6 | Agile
An improvement agreed in a retrospective has reduced the team's cycle time significantly. How can the team make sure the gain is sustained?
A) Make the practice part of the working agreement or definition of done
B) Stop measuring cycle time, since the improvement has already been achieved
C) Ask the project manager to remind the team of the practice every day
D) Try a completely different practice in the next sprint to compare results
= A
? Standardising a successful change — the "act" step of PDCA — embeds it in the team's way of working.

@@ BE6 | Hybrid
Which statement best describes the Kaizen approach to improvement?
A) Continuous, small improvements involving everyone
B) A large reorganisation carried out once every few years
C) Improvements designed only by external consultants
D) Waiting for a crisis before changing any process
= A
? Kaizen means continuous improvement through small, frequent changes in which everyone participates.

@@ BE6 | Predictive
A recurring defect in a manufacturing process has resisted several quick fixes. Which structured approach is best suited to finding and eliminating its root cause?
A) A Six Sigma DMAIC project
B) A new procurement process
C) A stakeholder engagement plan
D) A larger management reserve
= A
? DMAIC (Define, Measure, Analyse, Improve, Control) is designed to solve recurring process problems using data.

@@ BE6 | Agile
In a retrospective, the team reports poor coordination with the UX designers and suggests trying a shared Kanban board with them for two sprints. What should the scrum master do?
A) Support the experiment and agree how success will be judged
B) Reject the idea, since UX designers are not part of the Scrum team
C) Ask the product owner to decide whether the team may try the board
D) Implement the board permanently without waiting to see the results
= A
? Continuous improvement works through small, measurable experiments that the team owns and reviews.

@@ BE7 | Agile
The project is introducing a new digital tool across 30 sales offices. Which group should the project manager engage first to accelerate adoption?
A) Early adopters who can champion the tool
B) Laggards, so that resistance is eliminated first
C) Only senior managers, since others will follow them
D) Everyone equally, with a single announcement email
= A
? According to diffusion of innovation, early adopters influence the majority; enlisting them as champions accelerates adoption.

@@ BE7 | Predictive
The new warehouse process has been implemented and is working. According to Lewin's model, what is needed now?
A) Refreeze: reinforce the new process with policies and measures
B) Unfreeze: create readiness for the change by explaining why it is needed
C) Change: move people to the new process with training and support
D) Restart: return to the old process to compare results
= A
? Refreezing stabilises the new state so people do not slip back into old habits.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid
Employees affected by the closure of their old department say they miss the way things used to be and feel a sense of loss. According to Bridges' transition model, what should leaders do?
A) Acknowledge the endings and losses openly and support people through them
B) Focus only on the benefits of the new structure and avoid discussing the past
C) Tell the employees that their feelings are not relevant to the project
D) Accelerate the change so that the feelings have no time to develop
= A
? Bridges emphasises that transitions begin with endings; acknowledging losses helps people move through the neutral zone to a new beginning.

@@ BE7 | Agile
A new case management system will be released incrementally to 400 caseworkers. What should the training approach be?
A) Role-based training timed with each release, plus support
B) One full training course for all features before the first release
C) A user manual sent by email after the final release
D) No training, since the system is designed to be intuitive
= A
? Training aligned with incremental releases builds knowledge and ability just in time, while support reinforces adoption.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid
When communicating a major process change to employees, what message element is most likely to build their desire to participate?
A) What the change means for them and how they will be supported
B) The total budget and schedule of the project implementing the change
C) The names of the consultants who designed the new process
D) The technical architecture of the new supporting system
= A
? People engage with change when they understand its personal relevance ("what's in it for me") and the support available.

@@ BE8 | Agile
A new front-end framework is released that promises better performance. Some developers want to switch immediately, halfway through the release. What should happen?
A) Assess benefits, costs and risks with the team and product owner first
B) Switch immediately, since newer technology always improves the product
C) Ignore the framework completely, since the architecture was agreed at the start
D) Let each developer use the framework for their own stories if they want
= A
? Technological changes in the environment should be evaluated for value and risk, not adopted or ignored by reflex.

@@ BE8 | Predictive
New import tariffs will increase the cost of equipment the project plans to buy from abroad by 25%. What should the project manager do first?
A) Analyse the impact on budget and procurement and present options
B) Buy all the equipment immediately, before the tariffs take effect next month
C) Cancel the purchase and stop the project until the tariffs are removed
D) Reduce the quantity of equipment purchased without informing the sponsor
= A
? External economic changes are analysed for impact; options (alternative suppliers, timing, scope) go to decision-makers. Rushed or hidden decisions are inappropriate.

@@ BE8 | Hybrid
Public concern about the use of personal data has grown sharply after a competitor's data breach. The project is building a customer analytics platform. What should the project manager do?
A) Reassess privacy features and risks with stakeholders
B) Continue unchanged, since the project's legal obligations have not changed
C) Stop the project, since public opinion is against analytics platforms
D) Remove all data protection features to finish the platform faster
= A
? Social changes can affect value, risk and reputation. Re-evaluating privacy features and risks keeps the project aligned with stakeholder expectations.

@@ BE8 | Predictive
Two of the four qualified suppliers of a key material have merged, reducing competition. What should the project manager do?
A) Reassess the procurement strategy and related supply risks
B) Award the contract immediately to the merged company to secure capacity early
C) Ignore the merger, since the remaining suppliers are still qualified
D) Ask the merged company to split again so that competition is restored
= A
? Market consolidation changes procurement risks and leverage; the strategy should be reviewed accordingly.

@@ BE8 | Agile
An economic downturn forces the sponsor to cut the product budget by 30%. What should the product owner do?
A) Reprioritise the backlog for the most valuable outcomes within budget
B) Keep the same scope and ask the team to work faster to absorb the cut
C) Cut every feature by 30% so that all stakeholders share the reduction
D) Stop development entirely until the economy recovers
= A
? Adaptive planning responds to reduced funding by focusing on the highest-value work, rather than spreading cuts evenly or overloading the team.
